direction: down

title: |md
  ## epoll vs io_uring for sockets
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

epoll: "epoll (legacy, well-known)" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  e_body: |md
    epoll_create1(0) → epfd
    epoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, sock_fd, ...)
    epoll_wait(epfd, events, max, timeout)

    Edge-triggered or level-triggered.
    O(1) registration; O(events) wait.

    EPOLLEXCLUSIVE — multiple workers,
    one wakes per event (no thundering herd).
  |
}

iouring: "io_uring (modern)" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  io_body: |md
    Submit recv/send operations as SQEs.
    Kernel reports completions via CQEs.

    Net-specific ops (since 5.x):
    - IORING_OP_RECV / SEND
    - IORING_OP_RECVMSG / SENDMSG
    - IORING_OP_ACCEPT
    - IORING_OP_CONNECT
    - IORING_OP_RECVMSG_MULTISHOT (5.18+)

    Zero-copy variants:
    - IORING_OP_SEND_ZC
    - IORING_OP_SENDMSG_ZC

    Higher throughput, more complex.
  |
}

verdict: "When to pick" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  v_body: |md
    epoll: 80% of cases. Mature, simple, performant.
    io_uring: highest throughput, batch-y workloads,
              if you can pay the complexity tax.
  |
}

epoll -> verdict
iouring -> verdict
